\title{Propositional Self-Reference as a Collapse Principle\\
\large Classification, Decidability, and the Failure of a G\"odelian Analogy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a direct fixed-point model of self-reference in which a type $T$ is equivalent to a dependent product of propositions indexed by itself:
\[
T\simeq\prod_{x:T}P(x),\qquad P:T\to\Prop.
\]
Although the occurrence of $T$ on both sides suggests recursion, the proposition-valued codomain forces a complete collapse. Proof irrelevance makes the dependent product a subsingleton, so the equivalence makes $T$ a subsingleton. The empty case is impossible because a dependent product over an empty domain has a unique inhabitant. Consequently, the fixed points are exactly the types equivalent to a singleton. We derive decidable equality, exclusion of the Boolean type, mutual equivalence of all fixed points, cardinality one, and extensional constancy of every predicate on a fixed point. We give a finite cardinal-product algorithm and explain why genuine incompleteness requires coded syntax, effective substitution, and a specified decision problem.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Self-reference is central to logic and computation. Diagonal arguments construct sentences that encode claims about themselves; recursion theorems produce programs related to their own descriptions; and fixed points organize semantics for recursive definitions. These successes encourage a hazardous heuristic: if an object occurs in the domain of a construction used to define that same object, then logical complexity or undecidability should follow.

We test a direct form of that heuristic. Let $T$ be a type, interpreted as a collection of distinguishable objects, and let $P:T\to\Prop$ be a predicate. The dependent product $\prod_{x:T}P(x)$ consists of assignments providing, for every $x:T$, a proof of $P(x)$. We ask for an equivalence between $T$ and this product. Thus $T$ is identified with proposition-valued evidence quantified over $T$ itself.

The model behaves opposite to the anticipated G\"odelian picture. Its fixed points have no nontrivial internal variation. The source is proof irrelevance: two proofs of the same proposition are not distinct data. Two inhabitants of a product of propositions therefore agree at every coordinate and hence globally.

Our main theorem is the exact classification
\[
\left(\exists P:T\to\Prop,\ T\simeq\prod_{x:T}P(x)\right)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad T\simeq\one.
\]
This settles all cardinal and equality questions about the model and identifies the enrichments required for a nontrivial successor theory.

\section{Preliminaries}
A type $S$ is a \emph{subsingleton} if $\forall a,b:S$, $a=b$. It is nonempty if it contains an element. A nonempty subsingleton is equivalent to the singleton type $\one=\{\star\}$.

An equivalence $A\simeq B$ is a function with a two-sided inverse. Equivalences preserve emptiness, nonemptiness, subsingleton status, and cardinality. We reason extensionally about functions and assume proof irrelevance: any two proofs of the same proposition are equal.

Given a type $T$ and a family $F(x)$, the dependent product $\prod_{x:T}F(x)$ consists of dependent functions $f$ with $f(x):F(x)$ for every $x:T$.

\begin{lemma}[Propositional products are subsingletons]
For every predicate $P:T\to\Prop$, the type $\prod_{x:T}P(x)$ is a subsingleton.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Take $f,g:\prod_{x:T}P(x)$. For each $x:T$, the values $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ prove the same proposition, so proof irrelevance gives $f(x)=g(x)$. Function extensionality yields $f=g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The empty product]
If $T$ is empty, then $\prod_{x:T}P(x)$ has exactly one inhabitant for every $P:T\to\Prop$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There is a function from an empty domain into every family because no input case must be supplied. Any two such functions agree vacuously at every input.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Propositional self-referential fixed point]
A type $T$ is a propositional self-referential fixed point if there exist a predicate $P:T\to\Prop$ and an equivalence
\[
T\simeq\prod_{x:T}P(x).
\]
\end{definition}
The definition includes neither finiteness nor computability. ``Self-referential'' here means only that $T$ occurs as the object classified and as the product's index type.

\section{Structural classification}
\begin{theorem}[Subsingleton theorem]
Every propositional self-referential fixed point is a subsingleton.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $P$ and $e:T\simeq\prod_{x:T}P(x)$. The codomain is a subsingleton. Hence $e(x)=e(y)$ for all $x,y:T$, and injectivity of $e$ gives $x=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonemptiness theorem]
Every propositional self-referential fixed point is nonempty.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $T$ were empty, the product would contain the unique empty assignment. Applying the inverse of the alleged equivalence to that assignment would produce an inhabitant of $T$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete classification]
A type $T$ is a propositional self-referential fixed point if and only if $T\simeq\one$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In the forward direction, the preceding theorems make $T$ an inhabited subsingleton. Choose $t_0:T$. The constant function $T\to\one$ and the map $\star\mapsto t_0$ are inverse because every element of $T$ equals $t_0$.

Conversely, suppose $T\simeq\one$ and take $P(x)$ to be the always-true proposition. The product of these propositions is inhabited by the constant proof of truth and is a subsingleton, so it is equivalent to $\one$. Composing equivalences gives the required fixed point.
\end{proof}

\section{Consequences}
\begin{corollary}[Decidable equality]
Equality on every propositional self-referential fixed point is decidable.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Given $x,y:T$, the subsingleton theorem gives $x=y$. The decision procedure always returns the positive answer.
\end{proof}
The singleton is therefore a concrete counterexample to the claim that every fixed point of this form must be undecidable. Here decidability concerns equality of inhabitants. G\"odelian undecidability instead concerns algorithms deciding encoded propositions or derivability judgments.

\begin{corollary}[Boolean exclusion]
No type with two distinct elements, including the Boolean type $\{0,1\}$, is a propositional self-referential fixed point.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The subsingleton theorem would identify the two distinct elements.
\end{proof}
Thus the construction cannot retain one bit of information.

\begin{corollary}[Hierarchy collapse]
Any two propositional self-referential fixed points $A$ and $B$ are equivalent.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both are equivalent to $\one$; compose one equivalence with the inverse of the other.
\end{proof}
Iteration of this condition cannot create an arithmetical-style hierarchy: up to equivalence there is one level.

\begin{corollary}[Cardinality one]
Every propositional self-referential fixed point has cardinality exactly $1$. Among finite types, the condition holds precisely at cardinality $1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Cardinality is preserved by equivalence, and the singleton has cardinality $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Predicate collapse]
If $Q:T\to\Prop$ and $T$ is a fixed point, then for all $x,y:T$,
\[
Q(x)\Longleftrightarrow Q(y).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The subsingleton theorem gives $x=y$; substitution turns the two propositions into the same proposition.
\end{proof}
This applies to every proposition-valued observation on $T$, not only to the predicate witnessing the fixed point.

\section{Finite cardinal analysis}
Let $|T|=n$ and enumerate $T$ as $x_1,\ldots,x_n$. Each proposition $P(x_i)$ has truth cardinal $b_i\in\{0,1\}$. Therefore
\[
\left|\prod_{x:T}P(x)\right|=\prod_{i=1}^{n}b_i.
\]
For $n=0$, this is the empty product $1$. For $n>0$, it is $1$ exactly when all $b_i=1$ and otherwise $0$. The fixed-point equation
\[
n=\prod_{i=1}^{n}b_i
\]
therefore forces $n=1$ and $b_1=1$.

\begin{algorithm}[Finite witness test]
Given $n\geq0$ and $n$ truth values, initialize a product to $1$, replace it by $0$ upon encountering a false value, and compare the result with $n$. This tests one proposed predicate in $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Exhausting all $2^n$ predicates takes $O(n2^n)$ time. The classification theorem gives the optimized structural test: accept exactly when $n=1$.
\end{algorithm}

For $n=0$, the sole empty assignment has product cardinality $1$, not $0$. For $n=1$, false gives $0$ and true gives the unique witness of cardinality $1$. For $n=2$, the four assignments give $0,0,0,1$, never $2$. The same bound excludes every $n\geq2$.

\section{Why no G\"odel phenomenon follows}
A diagonal incompleteness argument requires more than occurrence of the same symbol on both sides of an equation. One needs an effective syntax represented by finite codes; substitution of codes into expressions; a diagonal lemma relating a formula to its application to its own code; a provability or truth relation connecting syntax with semantics; and a specified class of decision algorithms.

The present condition includes none of these. The right side is a semantic product of propositions, not a language of expressions. Its inhabitants are proofs, not codes. There is no operation by which a predicate inspects its own description. Proof irrelevance, rather than diagonalization, governs the equation.

One should distinguish \emph{index self-occurrence}, where $T$ indexes a family used to construct an object equivalent to $T$, from \emph{syntactic self-application}, where an expression receives a code of itself through effective substitution. Only the first appears here.

The proposed connection to the Church--Kleene ordinal $\omega_1^{CK}$ also requires correction. That object is an ordinal marking the boundary of computable well-orderings, not automatically the cardinality of a collection of types. Such a collection needs a universe, a coding, and a quotient before its cardinality is defined. For the present condition, quotienting by equivalence leaves exactly one class.

\section{Non-collapsing extensions}
\subsection{Data-valued fibers}
Replace $P:T\to\Prop$ with $F:T\to\Type$ and ask for
\[
T\simeq\prod_{x:T}F(x).
\]
Proof irrelevance no longer collapses the fibers. In the finite case the necessary equation is
\[
|T|=\prod_{x:T}|F(x)|.
\]
Nontrivial solutions now occur: a four-element domain with fiber sizes $2,2,1,1$ has product cardinality $4$. For finite sets, cardinal equality permits a bijection, although a useful theory should impose structural conditions preventing arbitrary tailoring. One may classify constant families separately from genuinely dependent ones and add computability, symmetry, or functoriality requirements.

\subsection{Coded syntax and semantics}
A G\"odelian replacement should define a recursively enumerable code type, partial interpretation, variables, and substitution. A diagonal operation should construct from a one-variable expression code $a$ a sentence code $d(a)$ satisfying a semantic relation of the form
\[
\operatorname{Meaning}(d(a))\Longleftrightarrow
\operatorname{Meaning}(a,d(a)).
\]
Undecidability theorems must then quantify over algorithms and state consistency, soundness, and representability assumptions explicitly.

\subsection{Universe-sensitive self-reference}
A collection of all types cannot simply be a member of itself without stratification. Introduce a universe of codes $C_u$ and a decoding map
\[
\operatorname{El}:C_u\to\Type_u.
\]
One must specify whether codes, decoded types, equivalence classes, or computable presentations are counted. To connect with $\omega_1^{CK}$, define a computable well-founded ordering on codes and prove the required rank, cofinality, and minimality properties.

\section{Applications and modeling lessons}
A product of propositions records simultaneous truth but not multiple witnesses. In knowledge representation it can certify every indexed assertion while forgetting how each assertion was established. In distributed systems, replacing local states by assertions can erase operational distinctions. In semantics, proof-irrelevant specifications support abstraction but cannot store computational choices.

For speculative models of cognition, the theorem gives a precise negative criterion. A state space intended to represent differentiated experiences must have distinguishable inhabitants. A construction forced to be a subsingleton cannot provide them. This conclusion constrains the model, not consciousness itself.

More generally, recursive equations should first be tested for invariant-preserving bottlenecks. If a type-forming operation always produces subsingletons, bounded sets, contractible spaces, or objects with trivial symmetries, then every fixed point inherits that restriction. Elementary invariants should be computed before recursion-theoretic intuition is invoked.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The classification depends on proposition-valued fibers, proof irrelevance, and a total dependent product. If proofs are distinguishable data, if products carry effects, or if fibers are arbitrary types, the collapse may fail. These alternatives are distinct mathematical models, not technical exceptions.

The immediate research tasks are to classify finite data-valued families; construct a coded language and prove a diagonal theorem before stating undecidability; and build a stratified code universe whose recursive expressions carry a rigorously defined ordinal rank. Homotopical fibers, coalgebraic unfolding, and resource-sensitive proofs offer further ways to preserve information. In every case, the first question remains: which invariants does the type-forming operation force on its fixed points?

\section{Conclusion}
The apparently recursive equation
\[
T\simeq\prod_{x:T}P(x),\qquad P:T\to\Prop,
\]
has a complete elementary classification. Proof irrelevance makes the product a subsingleton, so $T$ has at most one element. The empty case is impossible because the empty product has one inhabitant. Thus $T$ has exactly one element, and every singleton is a solution via the constantly true predicate.

Equality is decidable, the Boolean type is excluded, all fixed points are mutually equivalent, every fixed point has cardinality $1$, and every predicate on one is constant. The model supports neither an arithmetical hierarchy nor a Church--Kleene cardinality. Exact classification identifies the missing architecture: data, syntax, substitution, algorithms, and a semantic relation must be supplied before a G\"odelian analogy can become a theorem.

\end{document}

